#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44c68619-9cf3-5dd8-b3db-00c9730d3308" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Third Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "16e5cc04-aa74-55f7-992b-b9992a8dd1c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8632" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2bc15f0549ba8991d4e3aafc03284e526df24ba14ce336d788976f37b1c1ee4.png)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a $2\\times 2$ matrix that  could be the matrix $V.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=6$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-36I & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]-36[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 36I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/20/20ff5eb3c00c496739a4938d51819c523f35dd9631674b312b6603c9c998043a.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/ea/ea2f862b6140e7997710f842de24776363ea4bda60ecef64ded2a3b8f0710760.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/52/528da30da7d7781485d6c41d0dec0b5a02cc94bae1b27895908a2b470d262ef5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6732620-fad1-5dd3-98c5-07ba59d0b286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "bba077a6-56c6-5137-b949-26fae1c98625" :question/math-academy-id "e-8633" :question/problem "![](images/56/56923d0bc8f47d504837e3dca2f7191cf3d5d571245ee4453c8d1272dfb1dbd3.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a $3\\times 3$ matrix that could be the matrix $U.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=\\sqrt5$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ \nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have a third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n![](images/ad/adcd255d41a153210074a9ce091f40a65faeea884f2462275058ddf057b821f6.png)\n\nsuch that $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$ and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=1.$\n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} x_1 + \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} x_2 = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1 + 2x_2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n![](images/cb/cb7815fe5b5010a29a93a97bede16e26a7a0382a0d8c8f1c2a3af85762d24f00.png)\n\nwhere $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. Notice that the two linearly independent solutions are mutually orthogonal. Normalizing these vectors, we get \n\n![](images/bb/bbbb111ac90a0d42ab31aa78b0e36d2bdd00436e9f0fbabde2342120f32e1120.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c3/c348424c58cf218e1e4fc0eb3c3efa27517c6a24ac786146f0543f945f31af33.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "4fd6d412-d080-5fd1-9318-65a0f5f02dc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8634" :question/problem "![](images/92/925955a8375872fd58a5e319a41ea0642049113754cfaa45b2401cc78ed53773.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a vector whose components correspond to the $3$rd column of the matrix $U.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of an $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then \n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=7$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$\nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n![](images/c5/c5b5eec6439fb75addb666ceca926126e5a2be24f0f30dcdfbffd623023af51d.png)\nsuch that \n![](images/72/72487189cd6338fb35cdc88a7c9ca5937052fd1aa810015940c1559a0440c3c0.png)\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n![](images/b1/b1a4b3a15d573123e318cca21f79f71310e591b35ec7b591b44757aed136820d.png)\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n![](images/89/899ded4e181102a00313e42ecf2a914cb1f4a3a42293a2d6d785e71268abb446.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2999617053e39685151362c1e136c98577d66e579284aad43f906b4dee7269.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162045 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "566bda0f-f62d-581d-936d-baa8ae474e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Cases Requiring Orthogonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "1b9d8b05-cdb6-555b-b68c-b46000bb6650" :question/math-academy-id "e-8798" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c846fa0c1cf9ce3643f4a82b11d012a9675ead6f51c4a145816a2ca1201dea6.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a $3\\times 3$ matrix that could be the matrix $U.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=2$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$\nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n![](images/32/32e17465de4bba33105749d691af2ada0844faad000d5bb2ff8e8ed8d8e045e7.png)\n\nsuch that $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$ and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=1.$\n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2} x_1 + \\dfrac{1}{2} x_2- \\dfrac{1}{2} x_3 = 0   \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\sqrt2x_1 + x_2-x_3 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n![](images/eb/ebd9c8f2b5e96799c652c39f5859e702f9d069e2842099429d4640010c8882fa.png)\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n![](images/52/523cb0ebfa47497a9753cf1dd447db414aa20feb68380878cbe93d04ec407ad7.png)\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing the orthogonal vectors $\\mathbf{c}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U{:}$   \n\n![](images/6b/6bbe1a671b976508c3a5b25e11f0bec804ae0425afdec8ea5046130145eb69c0.png)\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n![](images/17/17552120ad9a7ef3e6a6b1170a88fc104758254f9f7d1ade70a7f7a833deac94.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6732620-fad1-5dd3-98c5-07ba59d0b286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de34356-e441-527f-8e4c-a10a700b1bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370656} {:answer/id #uuid "503b7b0f-0b44-52cc-ac26-f9ca356a42c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370657} {:answer/id #uuid "aae258de-8313-534f-84a3-46a6a22816e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370658} {:answer/id #uuid "df492e30-e34a-5117-a55e-8ad3cc025bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aae258de-8313-534f-84a3-46a6a22816e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370658} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2867685-0421-53b7-b86e-8ca6767a7db9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370655} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68ecc2d9-c685-5aaa-9523-09df62643625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370661} {:answer/id #uuid "8760a655-7b9b-5d5b-b1b5-beb654916343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370662} {:answer/id #uuid "b48fbf23-3099-582d-aa85-8b90e1c06ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/r